The core of the conflict is not the chores themselves, but a failure of the communication infrastructure. You are relying on verbal agreements that are subject to 'memory drift' and a social dynamic where one participant is withdrawing to avoid conflict. To resolve this, you must transition from an informal system based on trust and memory to a formal system based on transparency and documentation.

First, implement a shared digital or physical chore chart. This removes the 'disagreement about what the agreement meant' by making expectations explicit and visible. Use a rotating schedule rather than static assignments to ensure fairness over time, which accounts for shifting work schedules. 

Second, address the avoidance behavior through a structured house meeting. Rather than discussing specific missed chores (which can feel like an attack), frame the conversation around the 'system.' Propose the new tracking method as a way to reduce the need for repetitive, stressful conversations. This lowers the barrier for the avoiding friend to re-engage, as they are agreeing to a process rather than admitting fault.